{#
  The AICE tile grid - the way a hub page presents its set of onward links.

  tiles: [{ text, href, description, count }]

  Only the heading is a semantic link; `.defra-tile__link::after` stretches the
  click target across the whole tile, so screen readers get a concise link name
  while pointer users get the full card.

  The link carries `govuk-link` so it takes its colour, hover and focus states
  from the real component rather than from hand-copied hexes - which had drifted
  a whole rebrand behind the links beside them. `--no-visited-state` because
  these are the service's standing entry points: a hub whose tiles are all
  purple on second visit tells the user nothing.

  `count`, if set, renders as a tag after the tile heading - see `appNavCount`
  in the layout for the same idea in the navigation.
#}
{% macro appTileGrid(tiles) %}
  <ul class="defra-tile-grid">
    {% for tile in tiles %}
      <li>
        <div class="defra-tile">
          <h3 class="govuk-heading-s defra-tile__title">
            <a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state defra-tile__link" href="{{ tile.href }}">{{ tile.text }}</a>
          </h3>
          {% if tile.count %}
            <p class="defra-tile__count">
              <strong class="govuk-tag govuk-tag--red">{{ tile.count }} waiting</strong>
            </p>
          {% endif %}
          <p class="govuk-body-s defra-tile__body">{{ tile.description }}</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
{% endmacro %}
